.TH KEYRING-RSA 2
.SH NAME
keyring: RSApk, RSAsk, RSAsig, DSApk, DSAsk, DSAsig, EGpk, EGsk, EGsig \- specific public key systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "keyring.m";
keyring := load Keyring Keyring->PATH;
IPint: import keyring;

RSApk: adt {
    n:  ref IPint;    # modulus
    ek: ref IPint;    # exponent (encryption key)

    encrypt: fn(k: self ref RSApk, m: ref IPint): ref IPint;
    verify:  fn(k: self ref RSApk, sig: ref RSAsig, m: ref IPint): int;
};

RSAsk: adt {
    pk: ref RSApk;
    dk: ref IPint;    # exponent (decryption key)
    p:  ref IPint;
    q:  ref IPint;
    kp: ref IPint;    # dk mod p-1
    kq: ref IPint;    # dk mod q-1
    c2: ref IPint;    # 1/p mod q

    gen:     fn(nlen: int, elen: int, nrep: int): ref RSAsk;
    fill:    fn(n: ref IPint, e: ref IPint, d: ref IPint,
                p: ref IPint, q: ref IPint): ref RSAsk;
    decrypt: fn(k: self ref RSAsk, m: ref IPint): ref IPint;
    sign:    fn(k: self ref RSAsk, m: ref IPint): ref RSAsig;
};

DSAsk: adt {
    pk:     ref DSApk;    # p, q, alpha, key
    secret: ref IPint;

    gen:  fn(oldpk: ref DSApk): ref DSAsk;
    sign: fn(k: self ref DSAsk, m: ref IPint): ref DSAsig;
};

EGsk: adt {
    pk:     ref EGpk;     # p, alpha, key
    secret: ref IPint;

    gen:  fn(nlen: int, nrep: int): ref EGsk;
    sign: fn(k: self ref EGsk, m: ref IPint): ref EGsig;
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Keyring-gensk (2)
describes functions that generate public/private key pairs given an algorithm name
and a key length, and
.IR keyring-sha1 (2)
the signing of a digest with such a key.
These adts give the three older public key systems as numbers
.RB ( IPint s):
.BR DSApk ,
.B EGpk
and the three signature adts
.RB ( RSAsig
is one number,
.BR n ;
the others are
.B r
and
.BR s )
are declared in
.BR keyring.m .
.PP
.B RSAsk.gen
generates an RSA key.
.I Nlen
gives the length of the key modulus in bits;
.I elen
gives the public exponent's length in bits (it is odd and has that many bits:
FIPS 186-5 wants it above 2\u\s-216\s0\d, which 17 gives); and
.I nrep
is the number of repetitions of the Miller-Rabin primality test (0 gives the default, currently 18).
.PP
The RSA private key includes some extra values to speed computation.
.B Gen
provides those values but keys imported from other systems might not.
Given the essential numbers of an RSA private key,
.B RSAsk.fill
returns a key with the extra values.
It returns nil when
.I p
and
.I q
are equal or not prime,
.I n
is not their product, a value is negative,
.I e
times
.I d
is not 1 modulo the least common multiple of
.IR p \-1
and
.IR q \-1
(RFC 8017),
or
.I e
is 1 modulo it (such a key changes nothing).
A key's numbers may be at most 65536 bits long.
.PP
.B RSApk.encrypt
raises a message
.I m
to the public exponent, and
.B RSAsk.decrypt
and
.B RSAsk.sign
to the private one.
A message, ciphertext or signature must be a number from 0 to one less
than the key's modulus (RFC 8017): for any other the operations have no
result (nil), and a signature does not verify.
The private operation checks its result against the public key before returning it,
and has none when the private key's parts do not agree.
It is done in constant time for the key
(no step's time or address follows the primes, the exponents or the number operated on),
and on a number blinded with a random one drawn for each call.
.PP
.B DSAsk.gen
generates a DSA key.
If
.I oldpk
is not nil it takes the new key's modulus and group order from that key;
otherwise it generates a new pair of primes.
.B EGsk.gen
generates an ElGamal key with a modulus of
.I nlen
bits.
.PP
A DSA, ElGamal or ECDSA signature's nonce is made as RFC 6979 makes it,
from the private key, the number signed and 32 random bytes drawn for the signature
(the RFC's section 3.6):
a random number generator that repeats does not make two signatures with one nonce.
DSA and ECDSA sign in constant time for the key and the nonce;
ElGamal's arithmetic modulo
.IR p \-1
is general, on blinded numbers.
The
.B sign
functions have no result (nil) when the key's group is not one.
